A||N||7||Digisys||Body Panels||||5||
Z||000000||Digisys||08-12-2001||06:26 PM||||Hi there,<P>  I am looking for advice, tips, instructions, recommendations from anyone who may have swapped body panels and hardware from a 85 GT to a 88 Formula. I do not have any experience with exterior hardware on the Fiero but with some help I am sure I can do it. There are alot of questions, such as what hardware, braces, ; etc will I need to swap or if I should stock up on any fasteners before starting and so on. I just would like to not damage or break anything if I can avoid it !!! &lt;BG&gt;<P>  Thanxz,<BR>      TM||||reg||5
Z||000001||Songman||08-12-2001||06:30 PM||||My suggestion would be don't do it...<P>Your Formula is a car that is greatly admired by the Fiero masses. If you want the GT look, I would say to sell the Formula and buy a GT. Of course, it is your car and you can do whatever you want.<P>Changing body panels is not such a difficult thing. The main thing to remember is that you need all the 'underneath' stuff for the aero panels as well, like the impact absorber, the inner wheel wells, etc...<P>By the way... welcome to the forum!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||08-12-2001||10:38 PM||||Don't forget the metal brackets that are riveted(?) in the door sills. They hold up the side aero rocker panels. The Formula doesn't have 'em.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pontiaddict||08-13-2001||02:22 AM||||Well, since they didn't make the SE body style in 88, and 85 gt's don't have the 88 suspension, I'd say go for it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||PAUL88GT||08-13-2001||12:29 PM||||Please do not modify your Formula.  They are too rare. It will be worth more in the long run as stock Formula.  Sell the formula and buy an 85gt or 86se. <p>[This message has been edited by PAUL88GT (edited 08-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Digisys||08-13-2001||02:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B>My suggestion would be don't do it...<P>Your Formula is a car that is greatly admired by the Fiero masses. If you want the GT look, I would say to sell the Formula and buy a GT. Of course, it is your car and you can do whatever you want.<P>Changing body panels is not such a difficult thing. The main thing to remember is that you need all the 'underneath' stuff for the aero panels as well, like the impact absorber, the inner wheel wells, etc...<P>By the way... welcome to the forum!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the info and advice. Here is the story. I was told about an 88 Formula that was just sitting under a carport covered in dust. Ends up the guy who owned it ran the front end up under a truck, seared the headlights and shattered the hood. It still ran so I bought it (1200.00) It can be seen on my site <A HREF="http://www.diginostics.com." TARGET=_blank>www.diginostics.com.</A>  Then I went looking for a parts car and recently found the 85 GT Aero parked next to a barn covered in weeds seems it had been there since 1995.<BR>Good thing is all the body panels are still in good shape bad is the weatherstripping was shot letting water leak in to just about everywhere, and then the sun destroying anything the rust didn't. Soooo, to make a long story short.....I look at it as keeping <BR>2 cars from a worse fate. And I will end up with a unique 1988 GT Formula with Aero package and still all will be from original Fiero cars. What else could one want? &lt;BG&gt;<BR> Thanks to all for those that replied. <BR>I am thinking of having this place I found build me a motor. They sound real Good I'll check back with you all later on this. I'd like to do a little more probing first.<BR> <BR>  once again thanks<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000006||mrfixit58||08-13-2001||03:19 PM||||Does that odemeter say 15,500 miles or 115,500? If it's only 15,500. I vote that you try and return it to original. It'll be worth more. Low milage Fieros are hard to come by.<P>87 GT w/ 138,000 fun miles and counting...<P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||08-13-2001||07:28 PM||||Whichever way you decide to go you'll have a nice car. Good luck with it either way...<P>I'd still say fix it original and you'll have more in the long run. Parts to fix what you described shouldn't cost over a couple hundred bucks....<P>But just show us the pics when you get it done!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||14
